<figure class="cache-figure" data-script-occupancy>
 <p class="figure-title">A longer atomic operation makes unrelated readers wait</p>
 <label class="cache-field">Script execution <input type="range" min="1" max="100" value="20" data-cost aria-label="Script execution time" /><output data-cost-label>20 ms</output></label>
 <div class="script-schedule"><div class="script-row"><small>Script</small><div class="script-track"><span class="script-work" data-script-bar></span></div></div>{[0,1,2,3].map(i=><div class="script-row"><small>GET {i+1}</small><div class="script-track"><span class="script-wait" data-wait={i}></span><span class="script-work" data-work={i}></span></div><small data-delay={i}></small></div>)}</div>
 <p class="figure-note">0 ms → 110 ms · dashed: waiting · solid: executing</p>
 <p class="explanation" data-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
 <figcaption>The script starts at 5 ms. Four unrelated reads arrive at 8, 12, 16 and 20 ms; each needs 1 ms of execution. One serial execution lane, no network delay or other work. Times are illustrative, not Redis measurements. Increasing input size can enlarge an atomic operation's occupancy even if its source code stays short.</figcaption>
</figure>
<script>
import {scriptSchedule} from '../../lib/caching/atomicity.mjs';
document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-script-occupancy]').forEach(root=>{const input=root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-cost]')!;const bar=(selector:string,start:number,end:number)=>{const el=root.querySelector<HTMLElement>(selector)!;el.style.left=`${start/110*100}%`;el.style.width=`${(end-start)/110*100}%`;};const render=()=>{const cost=Number(input.value),s=scriptSchedule(cost);root.querySelector('[data-cost-label]')!.textContent=`${cost} ms`;bar('[data-script-bar]',s.start,s.end);s.reads.forEach((r,i)=>{bar(`[data-wait="${i}"]`,r.arrive,r.start);bar(`[data-work="${i}"]`,r.start,r.end);root.querySelector(`[data-delay="${i}"]`)!.textContent=`${r.start-r.arrive} ms wait`;});root.querySelector('[data-explanation]')!.textContent=`The script finishes at ${s.end} ms. The first GET waits ${s.reads[0].start-s.reads[0].arrive} ms before it can execute. Separate connections share this execution lane.`;};input.oninput=render;render();});
</script>
